rce wins via control over terminal states.\n\n- N8 → N1: N1 branches to N6, N0 (dead ends), N5 (→N6). From this, N6 or N0 ends the game. Player makes N6/N0 move → opponent can't move. So N1 path allows opponent to end with next move from opponent.\n\nThus, only move N8 → N3 directly ends the game at N9, a terminal node, where opponent has no move after — opponent loses immediately.\n\nTherefore, N3 is best. It results in immediate terminal node for opponent. This move is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N3</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6166666666666667, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N8, the legal moves are to N3, N4, or N1. We evaluate each move's outcome under the rules of vertex geography (last player to move to a dead-end node wins). \n\n- N8 → N3: leads to N9 (dead end), so opponent loses immediately → we win.\n- N8 → N4: leads to N7 (moves to N1, N2), then next player plays from N7. If opponent can reach a terminal node, they win. \n  From N7 → N1 leads into N6 (dead end), so if player reaches that path after opponent, they lose. However, N7 has two branches, so opponent can choose to go N1 → N5 (then N6) or N0 (end), which makes player lose.\n  Eventually, any route from N7 ends with N1, N5 → N6 or N0 → opponent controls terminal.\n  N4 path eventually leads to N7 → either N1/N2 (both dead ends), but only one move per turn. The path from here allows opponent to force wins via control over terminal states.\n\n- N8 → N1: N1 branches to N6, N0 (dead ends), N5 (→N6). From this, N6 or N0 ends the game. Player makes N6/N0 move → opponent can't move. So N1 path allows opponent to end with next move from opponent.\n\nThus, only move N8 → N3 directly ends the game at N9, a terminal node, where opponent has no move after — opponent loses immediately.\n\nTherefore, N3 is best. It results in immediate terminal node for opponent. This move is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N3</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe pl