irst move: N1 has no out-edges, and player loses only when no move exists on their turn.\n\nWe evaluate: both options lead to opponent losing. But moving N3 → N6 allows faster control of terminal trap.\n\nHowever, if opponent can't act from next state due to N1, it's already terminal on opponent's turn. So best to play toward a trap that cuts off opponent's options early.\n\nN6 → N1: opponent loses immediately after our action.\n\nTherefore, best move: N6.\n\n</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N3, the legal moves are to N5 or N6. We analyze each path for winning potential under optimal play.  \n\nN5 leads to N8 or N2:  \n- N8 moves to N9 or N7: terminal nodes with no outgoing edges (opponent loses). So if we force opponent into losing state, player wins.  \n- N2 leads to N9/N8 again—leads back to terminal moves. N9/N7 are terminal, so opponent eventually loses. All paths from N5 and onward lead opponent to losing states after a few turns.\n\nN6 goes to N0 or N1:\n- N0 → N4 → terminal, no further play; next turn opponent cannot move → we win.\n- N1: no outgoing edges; opponent loses immediately. So if we reach N1, opponent has no move.\n\nThus, from N3:\n- Move to N6 → opponent must move to terminal or N0 → either case, opponent eventually loses.\n- From N5: opponent may reach N8 → N7 or N9 (both terminal) eventually leads to opponent losing. So N5 leads to opponent eventually losing too.\n\nBut N6 gives immediate path to opponent losing at first move: N1 has no out-edges, and player loses only when no move exists on their turn.\n\nWe evaluate: both options lead to opponent losing. But moving N3 → N6 allows faster control of terminal trap.\n\nHowever, if opponent can't act from next state due to N1, it's already terminal on opponent's turn. So best to play toward a trap that cuts off opponent's options early.\n\nN6 → N1: opponent loses immediately after our action.\n\nTherefore, best move: N6.\n\n</reason>\n<