ing to N1 leads to N7, N0. N0 is a terminal (no outgoing edges), so the opponent loses immediately if they land there, but the opponent can only go to N7 or N0. If they go to N7, the chain continues to N6 or N3 (both terminal). So opponent loses by moving to N7 (as N6 and N3 are dead ends). So N1 leads to opponent eventually losing.<br>\n- Moving to N5 leads to N9, which leads to N0 — opponent loses immediately.<br>\n- Moving to N4 leads to N8 (terminal) or N1. N8 is terminal so opponent loses immediately.<br>\nAll moves lead to opponent losing eventually. But N4 leads directly to N8 (terminal) — opponent loses immediately.<br>\nThus, N4 offers the strongest guarantee of victory via immediate loss to opponent. <br>\nSo, best move is N4.</reason>\n<answer>moves to N4</answer>", "episode_score": -1.5, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N2, the legal moves are to N1, N5, N4. Let's evaluate each choice to see which leads to a winning position or forces the opponent to lose.<br>\n- Moving to N1 leads to N7, N0. N0 is a terminal (no outgoing edges), so the opponent loses immediately if they land there, but the opponent can only go to N7 or N0. If they go to N7, the chain continues to N6 or N3 (both terminal). So opponent loses by moving to N7 (as N6 and N3 are dead ends). So N1 leads to opponent eventually losing.<br>\n- Moving to N5 leads to N9, which leads to N0 — opponent loses immediately.<br>\n- Moving to N4 leads to N8 (terminal) or N1. N8 is terminal so opponent loses immediately.<br>\nAll moves lead to opponent losing eventually. But N4 leads directly to N8 (terminal) — opponent loses immediately.<br>\nThus, N4 offers the strongest guarantee of victory via immediate loss to opponent. <br>\nSo, best move is N4.</reason>\n<answer>moves to N4</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. After you move to a destination, the opponent acts from that destination. If that destination has no outgoing edges, the opponent loses. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N5\n\nDirected graph:\nN7 -> N11, N1, N9\nN3 -> N13, N9\nN6 -> N2\nN4: no outgoing edges\nN2: no outgoing edges\nN1: no outgoing edges\nN11: no outgoing edges\nN5 -> N7, N13, N3\nN9 -> N10\nN13 -> N0, N8\nN12: no outgoing edges\nN8 -> N4, N0, N6\nN10: no outgoing edges\nN0 -> N12\n\nLegal moves: N7, N13, N3assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Current node: N5, legal moves: N7, N13, N3. We analyze each branch for winning potential.\n\n- Move to N7: leads to N11, N1, or N9. N11 and N1 have no outgoing edges (opponent loses immediately). N9 → N10 (dead end). So opponent loses in any case → **win**.\n- Move to N13: leads to N0 or N8. N0 → N12 (dead end, opponent loses). N8 → N4 (dead), N0 (dead), N6. N6 → N2 (dead). So all moves lead to opponent losing or vacating. All branches terminate with opponent losing → **win**.\n- Move to N3: leads to N13 → N0 (opponent loses), N9 → N10 (opponent loses). All options are dead ends → **win**.\n\nAll moves lead to opponent losing immediately. Any move results in a win. Choosing one is fine. Pick first: N7.</reason>\n<answer>moves to N7</answer>", "episode_score": -1.5, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Current node: N5, legal moves: N7, N13, N3. We analyze each branch for winning potential.\n\n- Move to N7: leads to N11, N1, or N9. N11 and N1 have no outgoing edges (opponent loses immediately). N9 → N10 (dead end). So opponent loses in any case → **win**.\n- Move to N13: leads to N0 or N8. N0 → N12 (dead end, opponent loses). N8 → N4 (dead), N0 (dead), N6. N6 → N2 (dead). So all moves lead to opponent losing or vacating. All branches terminate with opponent losing → **win**.\n- Move to N3: leads to N13 → N0 (opponent loses), N9 → N10 (opponent loses). All options are dead ends → **win**.\n\nAll moves lead to opponent losing immediately. Any move results in a win. Choosing one is fine. Pick first: N7.</reason>\n<answer>moves to N7</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. After you move to a destination, the opponent acts from that destination. If that destination has no outgoing edges, the opponent loses. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N0\n\nDirected graph:\nN8 -> N9\nN7: no outgoing edges\nN0 -> N2, N4, N3\nN1: no outgoing edges\nN5: no outgoing edges\nN6 -> N8, N5\nN3 -> N2, N7\nN9: no outgoing edges\nN4 -> N1, N3\nN2 -> N6\n\nLegal moves: N2, N4, N3assistant\n", "response": "<reason>\nWe analyze from backward: nodes with no outgoing edges (dead ends) are winning positions for the player who owns them, since the next player loses.\n\nDead end: N7, N1, N5, N9. So any node that leads to one of these (or a cycle) is strategic.\n\n- N3 leads to N2 (looping) and N7 (loss to opponent). So N3 is good if one leads to N7, but N2 is a strong lead.\n- N4 → N1 (loss for opponent) or N3 (to N2). So N4 leads to a quick win (N1) or to N3 → N2 → N6 → N8 → N9 (loss). But N1 is terminal immediately so better.\n- N2 → N6 → N8 → N9 (terminal), so N2 leads to opponent’s loss.\n\nAll move chains: N0 → N2 → N6 → N8 → N9 (terminal opponent move loses), N0 → N4 → N1 (immediate opponent loss), N0 → N3 → N2 → N6 → N8 → N9.\n\nThus, every move from N0 leads opponent into a terminal node, so opponent loses. But N4 directly lead